<objects>
  <!-- Effect arrows, revived in #144.

       Every figure here is derived from vanilla's own effect arrow, Boomrose Arrow, which is the
       only one the game ships: BaseArrow / BaseArrowProjectile for the chassis, StrengthPenetration
       1 over 1d2 damage, and a commerce value of 0.20. Boomrose carries about 40-60% of the
       high explosive grenade mk I it imitates, and the gas and flashbang payloads below are scaled
       to their own grenade at the same ratio - same part as the grenade, dialled down.

       None of them carries a TinkerItem, which is also Boomrose's rule, and not a narrow one.
       Qud has exactly one crafting system and Tinkering is the ARTIFACT skill: it builds recovered
       old-world technology and nothing else. No arrow is craftable, but neither is a dagger, a
       long sword, a battle axe, a steel suit, a short bow or a compound bow. The Turbow is the
       only craftable bow in the game and it is the only one with an Examiner part - servos and an
       air turbine, an artifact that happens to be bow-shaped. Slugs and shells are craftable
       because they are cartridges serving firearms, which are themselves recovered tech.

       So these are reachable through the drop tables instead, merged into Ammo 2-4 in
       PopulationTables.xml. Giving them a recipe would not have filled a gap Freehold left; it
       would have put a stick into the artifact system, which vanilla declines to do for every
       wooden and forged object it ships. See #154 for the separate question of whether this fork
       should add a mundane crafting system of its own.

       All six also carry ExcludeFromTurretStock. Two arguments, and they cover different arrows.

       Three of the six burst - dream dew, starshell and stinger carry area payloads - and a turret
       the game stocked is one nobody chose the ammunition for. A target that closes to point blank
       puts the cloud or the flash on the turret and on anything beside it. Blaze, cryo and quill
       are single-target and that argument does not reach them.

       Note the scope carefully, because it is narrower than "turrets". Auto-stocking runs off a
       GenerateIntegratedHostInitialAmmo event, and only TurretTinker (wild turrets) and
       PlaceTurretGoal (an AI placing one) raise it. Tinkering_DeployTurret does not: a turret you
       deploy yourself arrives EMPTY and you load it from your own pack, so you pick what goes in
       and the hazard argument does not apply to it. Confirmed in game, not just read.

       What reaches all six is that they are hand-made and fragile in a way no vanilla arrow is. A
       quill lashed to a shank with sinew, a hollowed stinger with the sac still in it, a scored gas
       bulb, a phial, a wax shell - none of that survives being cycled through a magazine and a
       feed mechanism intact. Vanilla's arrows are a shaft and a metal head, which does.

       Blaze and cryo are the two whose grenades are thermal (HeatGrenade1 at TemperatureDelta 500,
       ColdGrenade1 at -400, both tier 1). Mura's original +/-400 matched those almost exactly, but
       the two directions need opposite treatment because Qud implements them differently.

       Heat takes the honest half: 250. Burning is threshold-gated AND self-extinguishing - the
       effect removes itself the moment temperature drops back under FlameTemperature, and
       Burning.GetBurningAmount keys damage off how far ABOVE that line you are (0-100 over is 1/turn,
       101-300 is 1-2, 301-500 is 2-3, rising to 5-6 past 900). So the size of the hit sets both the
       tick and the duration, and a small one is penalised twice: barely igniting something is worth
       about 2 damage total. At 250, two arrows put a target near 520 for roughly 20 turns of
       burning, which is what one thermal grenade does to a single target.

       Cold takes -50, well under half, because freezing has no damage tier at all - crossing the
       -100 brittle line is a binary immobilise. Halving the grenade would not moderate anything:
       from a starting 25, any hit deeper than about -125 is a one-shot disable, so -200 lands in
       exactly the same place as -400.

       -50 is three consecutive hits, and the arithmetic is worth writing down because -35 was
       play-tested at four to five and felt slower than it reads. Physics.IsFrozen is just
       Temperature <![CDATA[<=]]> BrittleTemperature, no save and no roll, so this is pure
       accounting - but temperature returns to ambient by Math.Max(5, |diff| * 0.02) every turn,
       and at these magnitudes the 0.02 term never reaches 5, so it is a flat 5 a turn. Each hit
       therefore nets -45, not -50, and every miss or idle turn hands 5 back:

           hit 1: 25 -> -20     hit 2: -20 -> -65     hit 3: -65 -> -115, frozen

       Note also that TemperatureOnHit passes Radiant: false, and on that branch cold resistance
       still applies whenever the result lands below 25. The glowpad this was tested against has
       ColdResistance 0; a resistant target will take proportionally less.

       Both stay on TemperatureOnHit rather than ThermalGrenade, single target by design. Fire
       already spreads on its own through the game's mechanics, so the blaze arrow needs no area
       part to reach more than what it hits, and the cryo arrow is meant to be a single-target tool.
       ThermalGrenade also sits on six objects in the game and every one inherits Grenade - it has
       no projectile precedent, unlike HEGrenade, GasGrenade, FlashbangGrenade, SunderGrenade and
       DeploymentGrenade, and its failure mode on a projectile would be silent.

       Names are Qud-era, not artifact-era. Qud gives grenades functional names because they are
       recovered old-world tech that arrives labelled - the Grenade blueprint carries an Examiner
       part and reads as UnknownGrenade until identified. Arrows have no Examiner at all: they are
       contemporary craft, and vanilla names them for the substance on the tip (boomrose, crysteel,
       fullerite), never for the effect. -->

